---
// About Cooking reference. The title, headings, order and step format follow the about-page
// skill (.agents/skills/about-page/SKILL.md). Numbers come from the data at build time.
import LabLayout from 'kp-design/layouts/LabLayout.astro';
import { ratios, blends } from '../../data/foundations';
import '../../styles/theme.css';
// Only the totals are used; the page is prerendered, so none of this reaches the browser.
import data from '../../../public/cooking-reference/pairings.json';

const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-US');
const built = new Date(data.built + 'T12:00:00Z').toLocaleDateString('en-US', { month: 'long', day: 'numeric', year: 'numeric' });
const unlabeled = data.recipes - data.labeled;

const steps = [
  {
    name: 'Findish collects recipes from independent cooks',
    where: 'GitHub Actions, once a week, in Findish',
    body: [
      `<a href="https://findish.now/">Findish</a> keeps a small index of recipes from ${data.sites} sites Kyle picked: each recipe's name, link, cuisine and the names of its ingredients, never the method. Ingredient names are matched to <a href="https://github.com/kyle-pace/kitchen-catalog">KitchenCatalog</a>, so “garlic cloves” and “minced garlic” both count as garlic.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Count which ingredients share recipes',
    where: 'This lab’s build script, run when Findish’s index has grown',
    body: [
      `The script reads that index and counts, for every pair of ingredients, how many recipes use both, on how many sites, and in which cuisines. The current counts are from ${n(data.recipes)} recipes as of ${built}, covering ${n(data.ing.length)} ingredients. The counts are saved with the lab.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Write the ratios and spice blends',
    where: 'This lab’s code, by hand',
    body: [
      `The ratio and spice blend pages are written for this lab, not collected from anywhere. There are ${ratios.length === 1 ? 'one ratio' : `${ratios.length} ratios`} and ${blends.length === 1 ? 'one blend' : `${blends.length} blends`} so far.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Your browser scores the pairings',
    where: 'Your phone or computer',
    body: [
      'When you pick an ingredient, the page works out each partner’s score from the counts and sorts them into bands. The calculator on a ratio page also runs in your browser. Nothing you pick or type is sent anywhere.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<LabLayout
  lab={{ name: 'Cooking reference', href: '/cooking-reference/' }}
  about="/cooking-reference/about/"
  title="About Cooking reference"
  description="What Cooking reference is, how the pairings are worked out and what it collects."
  image="/cooking-reference/og-about.png"
  imageAlt="About Cooking reference: what it is and how it works, on kylepace.dev."
  themeColor={{ light: '#f2f5f1', dark: '#131917' }}
>
  <article class="lab-page about-page prose">
    <h1 class="title">About Cooking reference</h1>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-title">
      <h2 id="what-title">What it is</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/cooking-reference/">Cooking reference</a> is for cooking without a recipe. It has three parts: pairings (what an ingredient
        is cooked with, drawn from thousands of real recipes), <a href="/cooking-reference/ratios/">ratios</a> (the proportions behind the
        classics, with a calculator) and <a href="/cooking-reference/blends/">spice blends</a> to make yourself.
      </p>
      <p>It’s for home cooks who already cook a little and want to understand why things work, so they can improvise.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="use-title">
      <h2 id="use-title">How to use it</h2>
      <p>
        Find an ingredient to see what goes with it. Add up to two more to see what goes with all of them. “Look at” narrows the
        counts to one cuisine, so you can see what pork goes with in Thai cooking rather than everywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        Partners come in bands. Strong means they’re cooked together a lot, by several cooks. Good is a clear habit. Worth trying
        shows up together now and then. Seen on one site only is listed apart and never ranked, since it may be one cook’s habit.
      </p>
      <p>Each ratio and blend page links to the pairings for its ingredients, and to recipes on Findish.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
      <h2 id="how-title">How it works</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        {
          steps.map((step) => (
            <li>
              <h3>{step.name}</h3>
              <p class="where">Where it runs: {step.where}.</p>
              {step.body.map((text) => <p set:html={text} />)}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="data-title">
      <h2 id="data-title">Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        The pairings come from the recipes <a href="https://findish.now/creators/">Findish searches</a>, which link back to each cook’s own
        site. Ingredient names come from KitchenCatalog, the list shared by Kyle’s kitchen apps. The ratios and blends are common
        cooking knowledge, written up here in our own words. Books that cover the same ground are listed on each page as further
        reading.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="good-title">
      <h2 id="good-title">Good to know</h2>
      <p>
        A pairing’s score has two parts: how far above chance the two share recipes, and how much of the picked ingredient’s
        cooking the other one is in. A few shared recipes count for less than many. In a combination, a partner’s score is its
        weakest pairing with anything you picked. “Mostly Thai” means three in five of the shared recipes that have a cuisine
        label carry that one.
      </p>
      <p>
        The recipes lean East Asian and toward baking, so some Western produce looks thin. {n(unlabeled)} of the
        {n(data.recipes)} recipes have no cuisine label, so the cuisine views see fewer recipes than the totals. Broad groups like
        “fresh herbs” are left out in favor of their kinds, and pantry staples (salt, sugar, oil, flour) are hidden unless you ask
        for them.
      </p>
      <p>Pairings say what cooks do, not why. Two ingredients can share recipes because they’re both in a common sauce.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
      <h2 id="privacy-title">Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        Analytics works the same as the rest of kylepace.dev: anonymous, no cookies, and you can turn it off (<a
          href="https://kylepace.dev/privacy/">privacy</a
        >). Events name the ingredients you pick by their catalog id, never anything you type. This lab doesn’t contact any other
        sites.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contact-title">
      <h2 id="contact-title">Get in touch</h2>
      <p>
        Questions or something that looks wrong: email <a href="mailto:mail@kylepace.net">mail@kylepace.net</a>. The code is public:{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/kyle-pace/cooking-reference">github.com/kyle-pace/cooking-reference</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</LabLayout>
